# Realtime Timer Invariants

Source of truth = **one row** in the `timers` table (`src/data/supabase/types.ts`).
Every client subscribes to realtime `UPDATE` events and **mirrors** DB state
into local React state.

## Owner vs viewer

```ts
const isTimerOwner = timer.host_id === userId;
```

Only the **owner** writes to the DB. Viewers are read-only — `Main.tsx` renders
just `<TimerDisplay>` for them. Never gate writes on anything other than this
ownership check.

## No echo loop — critical invariant

DB writes happen **only** from user-action handlers
(`startTimer` / `stopTimer` / `resetTimer` and the setting updaters).

The mirror `useEffect` in `useTimer.tsx` reads DB state into local state and
**must never write back**. Realtime events arriving from our own writes would
otherwise re-trigger writes and loop forever.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — mirror effect writes back
useEffect(() => {
  setLocalState(timer);
  if (timer.startTimestamp) updateTimerState({ ... }); // loop!
}, [timer]);

// ✅ GOOD — mirror is read-only
useEffect(() => {
  setLocalState(timer);
}, [timer]);
```

When adding new timer fields, route writes through existing user-action
handlers — don't add reactive writes inside subscription/mirror effects.
